<v Ira Glass>The utter cruelty of that last comment is just breathtaking. They do talk about leaving each other on the CD, but they never do leave each other. As Evan Harris says, the stages of quitting. OK. You think about it. You think it some more. Sometimes you quit. And in their case, you just keep thinking about it some more.
<v Ira Glass>最后那句话的残酷程度简直令人窒息。他们在光盘里确实谈到了离开彼此，但他们从未真正离开。正如埃文·哈里斯所说的，放弃的各个阶段。好吧。你想想看。再多想一想。有时你真的会放弃。而对他们来说，你只是不断地多想一会儿。

00:41:29.240 --> 00:41:32.410
<v Lisa Buscani>Part one, Work.
<v Lisa Buscani>第一部分，工作。

00:41:32.410 --> 00:42:13.180
<v Lisa Buscani>The morning began with weight. My head sown down to an unbearably heavy burden, pinned to the narcotic pillow. My eyes like their lids shut. Blindness would hinder my motion. My arms and legs remembered the heavy metal burned into their sense memory and refused me the grace I needed to get through the day. My body's refusal to function was in the name of survival. If I moved, that meant I would get up. If I got up, I would probably take a shower and get dressed. If I did that, I would go to work, to unspeakable, unthinkable horror.
<v Lisa Buscani>早晨带着沉重感开始。我的头耷拉着，承受着无法忍受的重负，被钉在麻醉般的枕头上。我的眼皮紧闭。失明会阻碍我的行动。我的手臂和双腿记住了烙印在感官记忆中的沉重金属，拒绝给我度过这一天所需的优雅。我的身体拒绝运作，这是为了生存。如果我移动，意味着我得起床。如果我起床，我可能会洗澡、穿衣。如果我做了这些，我就得去工作，去面对那无法言说、难以想象的恐惧。

00:42:13.180 --> 00:42:50.420
<v Lisa Buscani>Now, don't get me wrong. I have nothing against work per se. The concept of joining shoulder-to-shoulder with efficient, enthusiastic coworkers, discovering problems, crafting solutions, pushing onward, always onward via honest sweat of brow is attractive to me. I think of well-constructed women in business suits, the ones who really know how to tie a scarf, dashing through airports to catch that last flight to Rome, manhandling a potential empire crashing crisis, and spending an exhausted yet satisfied overnight in the company pensione.
<v Lisa Buscani>现在，别误会我。我并不反感工作本身。与高效、热情的同事并肩作战，发现问题，制定方案，推陈出新，始终通过额头上的辛勤汗水不断前进，这个概念对我很有吸引力。我想到那些穿着职业套装、干练的女性，那些真正懂得如何系丝巾的人，她们穿梭在机场赶往罗马的最后一班航班，应对可能导致帝国崩溃的危机，在公司旅馆里度过疲惫却满足的一夜。

00:42:50.420 --> 00:43:21.370
<v Lisa Buscani>I think of post office murals and 1950's communist propaganda. Strong, textured, geometric men in the stoic blue of the overall and the work shirt, reverently folding open the land, courageously braving the hell of the forge, lifting their ruddy cheeks and angular chins to God or government, simply, deeply, thankful for their job. That was work to me, not the salaried conflagration I went to every day.
<v Lisa Buscani>我想到邮局的壁画和 1950 年代的共产主义宣传。那些穿着工装裤和工装衬衫、线条硬朗的强壮男人，虔诚地开拓土地，勇敢地面对熔炉的地狱，将红润的脸颊和棱角分明的下巴抬向神明或政府，仅仅是深深地感谢自己的工作。那才是我心中的工作，而不是我每天去的那种领工资的灾难。

00:43:21.370 --> 00:43:58.440
<v Lisa Buscani>I worked at a small trade journal in the suburbs where I handled various layout and writing tasks. It was a tiny firm, a mom and pop organization, if you will, starring Leona Helmsley as mom. The publisher was a shrunken, shrewish woman in her 50's, the kind of woman who thought the word "broad" was a badge of honor. She fancied herself the last of the old Chicago newsies, barking her wisdom from her stuffy, windowless office, her cover sticks sweating off the gin blossoms in her nose, one of an endless supply of beige stained Virginia Slims dangling from her lipless mouth.
<v Lisa Buscani>我在郊区的一家小型贸易期刊工作，负责各种排版和写作任务。那是一家小公司，如果可以的话，就是那种“夫妻店”，由利昂娜·赫尔姆斯利担任“严母”。出版商是一位五十多岁、萎缩而刻薄的女人，那种认为“荡妇”这个词是荣誉勋章的女人。她自诩为最后一位老派芝加哥报贩，在她那闷热、没有窗户的办公室里咆哮着智慧，她的遮瑕膏掩盖不住鼻子上因酗酒留下的酒渣，嘴里总是叼着一支没完没了的米黄色 Virginia Slims 香烟。

00:43:58.440 --> 00:44:58.860
<v Lisa Buscani>Her motto was this-- yell, yell, and yell some more. If they're there the next morning, you've lost your touch. It was as if Satan had made a foray into human resources. She stood over my shoulder constantly, criticizing my work with raptorial fervor, waiting like a blood-mouthed hyena for the moment when I tripped up and made an error. And when I did screw up, as employees inevitably do when told they can do nothing else, she was all over me like drone bees to the queen. I was not capable of being right. The presumption that I could be right, that I could even have an opinion, infuriated her. Any argument I sputtered in my defense was dissected, mocked, and negated. After all, how can one question the wisdom of a woman who cornered the floral industry tabloid market? It was better to tip my neck open and let her drink.
<v Lisa Buscani>她的座右铭是——大喊大叫，再大喊大叫。如果他们第二天早上还在，说明你还没失准。这简直就像是撒旦对人力资源部门的一次突袭。她不断地站在我身后，以一种贪婪的热情批评我的工作，像一只嘴角带血的鬣狗一样等待着我出错的那一刻。而当我确实搞砸了——正如被告知什么都做不好的人必然会做的那样——她就像雄蜂围攻蜂王一样围攻我。我无法做对任何事。我居然认为自己能做对，甚至能有自己的观点，这让她感到愤怒。我为辩护而说出的任何话都被她剖析、嘲弄和否定。毕竟，怎么能质疑一个垄断了花卉行业小报市场的女人的智慧呢？还不如直接切开脖子让她喝血。

00:44:58.860 --> 00:46:03.190
<v Lisa Buscani>As horrible as it was, I couldn't just leave. She paid me $12,000 a year. Even with a second job on weekends, I barely got by. I rarely went out anywhere. I felt like restaurants were meant for other people. The world seemed to narrow to the places where I could and could not go. My friends nicknamed me Florence Nightingale. No one could nurse a drink like I could. I bought the cheapest food possible, the cheapest clothes available, and forgot about cutting my hair. My thought process was consistently reduced to the bottom line. Basically, the job taught me one thing-- how to live a life with little or no option. And after a while, I was incapable of imagining myself in any other circumstance. Forget about my college degree and all the experience I obtained. This was how it was and always would be. If you thought about it too much, it became hard to breathe.
<v Lisa Buscani>尽管这很可怕，但我不能就这么离开。她一年付给我 12,000 美元。即使周末有第二份工作，我也勉强糊口。我很少去任何地方。我觉得餐馆是为别人准备的。世界似乎缩小到我能去和不能去的地方。朋友们给我起了个外号叫“南丁格尔”。没人能像我那样熬日子。我买最便宜的食物，穿最便宜的衣服，连理发都忘了。我的思维过程持续地降格为底线。基本上，这份工作只教会了我一件事——如何过一种几乎没有选择的生活。过了一段时间，我无法想象自己身处任何其他环境中。忘记我的大学学位和获得的所有经验吧。事实就是这样，而且永远会是这样。如果你想得太多，连呼吸都变得困难。

00:46:03.190 --> 00:46:45.640
<v Lisa Buscani>The breaking came when review time rolled around, two months later than she said it would when she hired me. She told me she wasn't going to give me a raise. Raises were given on merit in her office, she said. My work did not merit a raise. The room was suddenly very literally light. It was almost too bright in the room to look at her without wincing. I could hear the hum of the office lamps. I was amazed at my calm. I stood, walked to my desk, took my office keys off the chain, and walked out the door.
<v Lisa Buscani>爆发发生在绩效评估时，比她雇我时说的时间晚了两个月。她告诉我，她不会给我加薪。她说，在她的办公室里，加薪是看表现的。我的工作不值得加薪。房间突然变得非常明亮。房间里亮得让人睁不开眼，甚至无法直视她。我能听到办公室灯管的嗡嗡声。我对自己感到震惊，我竟然如此冷静。我站起来，走到办公桌前，从链子上取下办公室钥匙，然后走出大门。

00:46:45.640 --> 00:47:30.390
<v Lisa Buscani>I did not know what I was going to do and, blessedly, I did not care. I sat quietly on the train back to the city, feeling my back muscles slowly unravel and my stomach lift six inches into my heart. The warmth started in my cheeks and spread down to my fingers, down to my feet. My lungs expanded completely, which hurt a bit. It felt like I had been swimming all day and had forgotten to breathe. I didn't mind the pain. Peace is a fine travelling companion.
<v Lisa Buscani>我不知道自己要做什么，但幸运的是，我不在乎。我安静地坐在回城的火车上，感觉到背部肌肉慢慢放松，胃部向上提了六英寸，贴近了心脏。温暖从脸颊开始，蔓延到手指，蔓延到脚趾。我的肺部完全舒展了，这还有点疼。感觉就像我游了一整天的泳，忘了呼吸。我不介意这种疼痛。宁静是一个很好的旅伴。

00:47:30.390 --> 00:47:41.890
<v Ira Glass>Lisa Buscani. More from her in a minute. But first this song from Robert Metrick, first performed in Chicago's Club Lower Lengths. You're listening to Your Radio Playhouse.
<v Ira Glass>丽莎·布斯卡尼。一会儿还有更多她的内容。但首先是这首来自罗伯特·梅特里克的歌，首次在芝加哥的 Club Lower Lengths 表演。你正在收听的是《你的广播剧场》。

00:47:41.890 --> 00:50:39.100
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "THE CALENDAR SONG" BY ROBERT METRICK]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 罗伯特·梅特里克的《日历之歌》]

00:50:39.100 --> 00:50:43.960
<v Lisa Buscani>Part two, Love.
<v Lisa Buscani>第二部分，爱。

00:50:43.960 --> 00:51:03.870
<v Lisa Buscani>When we started, he and I, I was thin. My face was a diamond, all clean walls. Nothing held me back. Not my clothes, not my lack of money. I was weightless but I had control, like astronauts after they learn to swim properly in space.
<v Lisa Buscani>刚开始的时候，我和他，我很瘦。我的脸像一颗钻石，轮廓清晰。没有什么能阻挡我。不是我的衣服，也不是我没钱的事实。我处于失重状态，但我拥有控制权，就像宇航员学会如何在太空中游泳之后一样。

00:51:03.870 --> 00:51:26.580
<v Lisa Buscani>Our conversations used to overlap and dovetail and interweave, and occasionally we'd stop to breathe. Great pictures of limitless futures, expensive and unfettered. That's how we talked it. I tried to break my visions down to see how to make them happen and he just made his bigger, which is one way of doing it, I guess.
<v Lisa Buscani>我们的谈话曾经重叠、衔接、交织，偶尔我们停下来喘口气。关于无限未来的宏伟图景，昂贵且不受束缚。我们就是这样谈论的。我试着把我的憧憬拆解开来，看看如何实现它们，而他只是把他的憧憬放大，我想这也是一种方式。

00:51:26.580 --> 00:51:52.630
<v Lisa Buscani>When we made love, we knew how to forget ourselves. That, I'm convinced, is passion. To kiss him so hard and hold him so hard that the act itself is forgotten. And all that is remembered is skin and hair and warmth. That's a gift. That's something we kept for a long, long time.
<v Lisa Buscani>当我们做爱时，我们知道如何忘记自我。我坚信，这就是激情。紧紧地拥吻他，以至于动作本身都被遗忘了。剩下的只有皮肤、头发和温暖。那是一份礼物。那是我们保留了很长很长时间的东西。

00:51:52.630 --> 00:52:16.840
<v Lisa Buscani>The decline was relatively slow. Every time we fought, I took the opportunity to shift the relationship from one shoulder to another just to give myself a break and better balance. And we'd start again. I had carried us a long time. He could say that, too, I suppose.
<v Lisa Buscani>衰退是相对缓慢的。每次我们争吵，我都会抓住机会把这段关系从一个肩膀换到另一个肩膀，只是为了给自己一个喘息的机会，保持平衡。然后我们又重新开始。我背负了我们很长一段时间。我想他也可以这么说。

00:52:16.840 --> 00:52:42.350
<v Lisa Buscani>I started gaining weight, and that seemed to be the theme of it. It became harder and harder to get down to the bone. The way we were put together began to escape me. When we made love, we suddenly knew exactly where we were and what was going on. It was one of those great sadnesses that you carry in your throat.
<v Lisa Buscani>我开始发胖，这似乎成了主题。想要触及本质变得越来越难。我们结合在一起的方式开始离我而去。当我们做爱时，我们突然清楚地知道我们在哪里，发生了什么。那是你喉咙里承载的巨大悲伤之一。

00:52:42.350 --> 00:52:58.960
<v Lisa Buscani>I couldn't quit it because this was one so easy. On the morning of the first night he stayed with me, I woke up and looked at him and found home. I could fold myself into him, into the warmth found in those deco corners.
<v Lisa Buscani>我无法放弃它，因为这段关系曾经如此简单。在他和我共处的第一晚后的早晨，我醒来看着他，找到了家的感觉。我可以让自己融入他，融入那些装饰角落里发现的温暖。

00:52:58.960 --> 00:53:15.180
<v Lisa Buscani>I couldn't quit it because I had been alone much of my life. To be alone again meant an energy my aging had confiscated. And even though I was still alone with him, a future was the crumb of consolation.
<v Lisa Buscani>我无法放弃它，因为我生命的大部分时间都是孤独的。再次孤独意味着一种我随着年龄增长而丧失的能量。尽管我和他在一起时仍然感到孤独，但未来却是一点点安慰。

00:53:15.180 --> 00:53:29.360
<v Lisa Buscani>I couldn't quit it because it didn't fit the only picture I had. My parents knew the necessary work of love. They were the model here. If they could work dogged for their heart, so could I, I thought.
<v Lisa Buscani>我无法放弃它，因为它不符合我唯一拥有的图景。我的父母知道爱的必要工作。他们是这里的榜样。我想，如果他们能为了彼此的心而顽强地努力，我也能。

00:53:29.360 --> 00:53:37.390
<v Lisa Buscani>I couldn't quit it because of winter. I can't stand to sleep alone in the cold.
<v Lisa Buscani>我无法放弃它，因为冬天。我无法忍受在寒冷的夜晚独自睡觉。

00:53:37.390 --> 00:54:29.170
<v Lisa Buscani>The end happened several times, actually. We would inevitably hook up again and mess around. We just didn't look in each other's eyes is all. One night we were eating dinner and his smile was less than honest. And I thought, well, then this is it. I said, I've been working far too hard for far too long. And he agreed, and I wanted him to fight it, and was disappointed by how light he was. Smiling, so ready to fly. I felt light, too, but it was the way you are before you catch a cold, all unfocused and buzzing. And the chill comes from inside.
<v Lisa Buscani>结束其实发生了好几次。我们不可避免地会再次复合，继续纠缠。我们只是不再直视对方的眼睛而已。一天晚上我们正在吃晚饭，他的笑容显得不那么真诚。我想，嗯，就是这样了。我说，我工作得太努力太久了。他同意了，我本希望他能反抗，但他表现得如此轻盈，让我感到失望。微笑着，随时准备飞走。我也感到轻盈，但这就像你感冒前的那种状态，晕乎乎的，嗡嗡作响。那种寒冷来自内心。

00:54:29.170 --> 00:54:31.550
<v Ira Glass>Lisa Buscani.
<v Ira Glass>丽莎·布斯卡尼。

00:54:31.550 --> 00:54:43.670
<v Ira Glass>Dwight Okita has this poem about quitting from his book Crossing with the Light . He wrote it back when he worked as a dance instructor. It's about a student he became friends with.
<v Ira Glass>德怀特·奥基塔在他的书《随光穿行》中有一首关于放弃的诗。这是他当舞蹈教练时写的。那是关于他的一位朋友学生。

00:54:43.670 --> 00:55:12.190
<v Dwight Okita>Somewhere in Chicago, a woman unplugs a toaster from a wall, and suddenly her apartment is empty. She wraps the cord jump rope style into a bow, lowers the appliance into a box marked "Kitchen Things," and tapes it shut. All day, boxes move past her, a brown blur against the white walls. How many men does it take to lift a woman's spirits?
<v Dwight Okita>在芝加哥的某个地方，一个女人拔掉了墙上的烤面包机插头，突然她的公寓空了。她把电线像跳绳一样绕成一个蝴蝶结，把电器放进标着“厨房用品”的盒子里，然后用胶带封好。整天，箱子从她身边移过，白墙映衬出一道棕色的模糊影像。要多少男人才能振奋一个女人的精神？

00:55:12.190 --> 00:55:38.130
<v Dwight Okita>"Make your arms like a barrel," I scolded, your dance instructor, fox-trotting you around the room. "Women are always walking backwards, aren't they?" you said, looking at your feet. And I spun you. "No, just going in circles." And here we laughed. In the mirrors of the dance studio, we laughed. And I saw us, lost in the fun house again. I want it always to be fun.
<v Dwight Okita>“把你的双臂摆成桶状，”作为舞蹈教练的我责备道，带着你在房间里跳狐步舞。“女人总是倒着走，不是吗？”你看着自己的脚说道。我让你旋转起来。“不，只是在绕圈子。”我们大笑起来。在舞蹈室的镜子里，我们大笑。我看着我们，再次迷失在游乐场里。我希望它永远如此有趣。

00:55:38.130 --> 00:55:58.120
<v Dwight Okita>Now everything is loaded on the truck. She sits behind the wheel of something larger than her. "I'm going to Timbuktu and I'm taking my time," she says her hands on the wheel. "Peaches, pears, apples, plums. Tell me when your birthday comes." And I wave to her from the curb.
<v Dwight Okita>现在一切都装上了卡车。她坐在一辆比她大得多的驾驶座后面。“我要去廷巴克图，我会慢慢来，”她双手握着方向盘说。“桃子、梨子、苹果、李子。告诉我你的生日是什么时候。”我从路边向她挥手。

00:55:58.120 --> 00:56:18.680
<v Dwight Okita>It paints a sad picture, this woman in a van pulling out of a driveway no longer hers. And for her, I do a farewell samba on the lawn, alone, taking the darkness in the crescent of my arms, leading it in a dance I'm just beginning to learn.
<v Dwight Okita>这描绘了一幅悲伤的画面，这个女人开着货车驶出一个不再属于她的车道。而对她，我在草坪上独自跳起告别的桑巴，拥抱怀抱中那弯弯的黑暗，引领着它跳起我刚开始学习的舞蹈。

00:56:18.680 --> 00:56:21.640
<v Ira Glass>"The Farewell Samba" by Dwight Okita.
<v Ira Glass>德怀特·冲田（Dwight Okita）的《告别桑巴》。

00:56:21.640 --> 00:56:41.700
<v Ira Glass>And we're going to close our broadcast today with a piece by the late Philip Larkin, which seems perfect as you contemplate upcoming quits. Over the course of the next week, we're not quitting anything. I think that's what you've gotten out of this particular program. Anyway, this is called "Poetry of Departures."
<v Ira Glass>节目最后，我们将以已故诗人菲利普·拉金（Philip Larkin）的一首诗作为结束，这首诗似乎非常适合你在思考即将到来的离职时阅读。在接下来的一周里，我们不会放弃任何事情。我想这就是你们从这个特定节目中得到的启示。总之，这首诗名为《离别的诗意》。

00:56:41.700 --> 00:56:53.480
<v Ira Glass>Sometimes you hear, fifth-hand/ As epitaph/ He chucked up everything and just cleared off,/ And always the voice will sound/ Certain you approve/ This audacious, purifying,/ Elemental move.
<v Ira Glass>有时你从第五手传闻中听闻/ 作为墓志铭/ 他抛弃了一切，孑然一身远走高飞/ 而那声音总是听起来/ 笃定你会赞赏/ 这种大胆、纯粹/ 本原的举动。

00:56:53.480 --> 00:57:05.330
<v Ira Glass>And they are right, I think./ We all hate home/ And having to be there/ I detect my room,/ It's specially-chosen junk,/ The good books, the good bed,/ And my life, in perfect order/ So to hear it said/
<v Ira Glass>我想，他们说得对。/ 我们都讨厌家/ 讨厌不得不待在那里/ 我审视着我的房间/ 那些精挑细选的破烂/ 好书，好床/ 以及我那井井有条的生活/ 所以当听到那句/

00:57:05.330 --> 00:57:21.720
<v Ira Glass>He walked out on the whole crowd/ Leaves me flushed and stirred,/ Like Then she undid her dress/ Or Take that, you bastard;/ Surely I can, if he did?/ And that helps me stay/ Sober and industrious./ But I'd go today,/
<v Ira Glass>他抛下众人离去/ 让我感到面红耳赤，心潮澎湃/ 就像“然后她解开了裙子”/ 或“接招吧，你这混蛋”；/ 他能做到，我当然也能，不是吗？/ 那让我保持/ 清醒和勤奋。/ 但我今天就会走，/

00:57:21.720 --> 00:58:27.820
<v Ira Glass>Yes, swagger the nut-strewn roads,/ Crouch in the fo'c'sle/ Stubbly with goodness, if/ It weren't so artificial,/ Such a deliberate step backwards/ To create an object/ Books; china; a life/ Reprehensibly perfect.
<v Ira Glass>是的，在撒满坚果的路上大步流星，/ 蜷缩在船头/ 满脸胡茬，充满善意，如果/ 这不是如此虚假，/ 不是为了创造某种客体/ 而刻意倒退一步/ 书籍；瓷器；一种/ 无可指责的完美生活。

00:58:27.820 --> 00:58:33.110
<v Evan Harris>There's so many famous quits. There's so many quits that have driven world history.
<v Evan Harris>有太多著名的离职案例。有太多的离职行为推动了世界历史的发展。

00:58:33.110 --> 00:58:38.110
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. See you next week with more stories of This American Life .
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。下周再见，届时将有更多《美国生活》（This American Life）的故事。
